  "difficulty": 2,
  "prompt": "Measuring in degrees, what is the period of $y = \\cos(4x)$? Give just the number of degrees.",
  "answerType": "numeric",
  "primaryAnswer": "90",
  "alternativeAnswers": [],
  "acceptanceMode": "normalized",
  "hint": "In degrees, the period of $y = \\cos(bx)$ is $\\frac{360^\\circ}{b}$.",
  "hints": [
    {
      "level": "nudge",
      "text": "In degrees, the period of $y = \\cos(bx)$ is $\\frac{360^\\circ}{b}$."
    },
    {
      "level": "guide",
      "text": "Here $b = 4$, so compute $\\frac{360}{4}$."
    },
    {
      "level": "reveal",
      "text": "$\\frac{360}{4} = 90$, so the period is $90^\\circ$."
    }
  ],
  "explanation": [
    "A basic cosine wave repeats every $360^\\circ$.",
    "The factor of 4 inside makes the input run four times as fast, packing four cycles into that same space.",
    "Each cycle is therefore $\\frac{360^\\circ}{4} = 90^\\circ$ wide."
  ],
  "alternativeExplanations": [
    {
      "title": "Count the cycles",
      "steps": [
        "If four complete waves fit between $0^\\circ$ and $360^\\circ$, each one takes a quarter of that stretch.",
        "A quarter of $360^\\circ$ is $90^\\circ$."
      ]
    }
  ],
